\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Indian  \highlight22\uc2 fx \highlight\uc2  reserves top $90 bln on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 .  \b0\par\par\uc2 423 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Oktober 2003\par 12:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BOMBAY, Oct 18 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves surged to a record $90.35 billion in the week to October 10 on the back of heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, traders and economists said.  \par \par \uc2 Data released by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Saturday showed that the reserves were up $2.61 billion during the week. So far in 2003, the reserves are up by more than $20 billion, with average weekly accretions at more than half a billion dollars.  \par \par \uc2 "The RBI (Reserve Bank of India) was very aggressive in the market last week, which could account for the huge reserves build-up," said a trader at a state-run bank.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could have absorbed dollar inflows, which had come into the market in earlier weeks as well. Portfolio inflows were strong, and there could have been some deposit inflows. Trade inflows were minimal."  \par \par \uc2 There could have been re-investments in the local market, following the $5.2 billion redemption of the Resurgent India Bonds issued to overseas Indians earlier this month.  \par \par \uc2 "Part of that money could have been re-invested in local bank deposits, or mutual funds, as overall returns are good with Indian yields attractive and the rupee strong," said Saumitra Choudhuri, economic advisor to rating agency ICRA Ltd, local affiliate of Moody's Investors Service.  \par \par \uc2 Strong inflows had helped the rupee climb to a three-year closing high of 45.26/27 against the dollar on October 9, taking gains for calendar 2003 to six percent. But \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pulled it back. The rupee closed Friday at 45.34/34.  \par \par \uc2 Still, the country's growing economy may continue to draw portfolio investments, which could lift the rupee and further add to the reserves.  \par \par \uc2 Foreign funds have already pumped in more than $4 billion so far in 2003, which is about five-and-half times the investments they made in the whole of 2002.  \par \par \uc2 "The growth story is very good, which will keep portfolio investments strong for some more time," Choudhuri said. "Exports of services are also quite strong, and should offset outflows towards imports, which will support the rupee."  \par \par \uc2 Investors expect Asia's third largest economy to grow by more than six percent this year and sustain its momentum next year as it benefits from good rains, low interest rates, strong consumption and rising investment spending.  \par \par \uc2 Growth had shrunk to 4.3 percent last year from 5.6 percent the previous year, because of a severe drought.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031018dzai002p9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
