\par\par \b \uc2 WRAP: India Rupee Breaches Key 45.50/Dlr Resistance Fri  \b0\par\par\uc2 457 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2003\par 08:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--Spurred by continuing strong capital flows, the Indian rupee broke key resistance of INR45.50 against the dollar in morning trading Friday, a level many in the market recently believed would only be reached by the end of the year.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  watchers now say the rupee is heading for INR44.80 against the dollar by year-end, a level that would be a 6.5% rise since the start of 2003. So far this year, it has risen 5.2%.  \par \par \uc2 In morning trading Friday, the rupee touched an intraday high of INR45.45, its highest since Aug. 8, 2000.  \par \par \uc2 It closed Wednesday at INR45.57.  \par \par \uc2 Traders attributed Friday's particularly strong gains to trade and portfolio inflows that accumulated during Thursday's national holiday. Furthermore, the rupee's gains have been stronger in recent weeks after a Sept. 20 statement from the Group of Seven leading industrial nations called for more flexible exchange rates - interpreted by many as a call to drop support for the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "By and large the dollar will remain weak in the coming months, and capital inflows to India will continue," says Surendra Rosha, head of treasury sales at HSBC India, forecasting the rupee at around INR44.80 by the end of the year.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Dlr At INR45.25 Next Target \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On a nearer horizon, INR45.25 appears to be the market's next target, says IndusInd Bank's treasurer Sharukh Wadia, who expects that level to be tested by the end of this month.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's exporters reiterated Friday their concerns over the country's competitiveness in global markets from the sharply rising rupee. "We want the government to do something now otherwise it will hurt our competitiveness very badly," says M. Rafeeque Ahmed, President of the Federation of Indian Export Organisations.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The strong rupee has slowed export growth over the past several months, with shipments rising only 4.2% on the year in August. By comparison, exports rose 20% in the same month of 2002.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In recent months, trade and business organizations have repeatedly called on the Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the spot \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to slow the rupee's rise.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RBI Deputy Governor Rakesh Mohan said in a television interview Thursday that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would only \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem excessive exchange-rate volatility - a statement that monetary officials have repeated several times in recent months.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, many in the market believe the RBI may not resist the rupee's rise as long as it is driven by fundamentals, such as capital inflows and the dollar's global weakness.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Anil Varma, Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 22884212; anil.varmadowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Alastair McIndoe  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031003dza30006u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
