\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee ends flat, off peak as cbank buys dlr. \b0\par\par\uc2 566 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 August 2003\par 13:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     BOMBAY, Aug 28 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee          ended\par flat on Thursday, easing from an intra-day high as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bought dollars through state-run banks, dealers said.\par     The rupee ended at 45.8475/8575 per dollar, off an\par intra-day high of 45.81 and nearly steady from the previous\par close 45.84/85.\par     It is up nearly 0.11 percent since Monday, when two car\par bombs rocked the country's financial capital, killing 52 people\par and injuring about 150 others.\par     "Dollar inflows from exporters and foreign funds were good.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  But \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ensured that the rupee gave up \par \par \uc2  its gains," said a dealer at a state-run bank. "It's unlikely \par \par \uc2  that the rupee will break the 45.82 level in the next few \par \par \uc2  days." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  usually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  market to contain sharp swings and maintain the country's \par \par \uc2  export competitiveness. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     The rupee had weakened slightly in the morning on technical\par factors.\par     Banks routinely sell dollars on Wednesdays and buy them\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par back before the weekend to benefit from a swap differential of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par nearly 1.50 paise. The settlement for Thursday's spot deals has\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par been shifted to Tuesday because of a holiday in New York on\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders said there were sporadic dollar purchases by a\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par state-run oil company. Oil companies routinely buy dollars at\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the month-end to pay for imports.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the rupee has been drawing support from continued\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par dollar inflows from foreign investors, who have stepped up\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par their exposures to Indian stocks on expectations of stronger\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par growth.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par India's economy, Asia's third largest, is likely grow more\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par than six percent this year due to increased farm output and\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par demand for manufactured goods, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  forecast on\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     The economy expanded 4.3 percent in the past financial year\par to March.\par     Robust foreign fund purchases of shares have helped the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par rupee gain 4.65 percent against the dollar this year. They have\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par also been the key driver of the stock market, which has risen\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 44 percent from a six-month low hit in late April.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Equity purchases by overseas investors between January 1\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par and August 26 totalled nearly $2.15 billion, almost thrice the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par $740 million invested in the whole of 2002.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FORWARDS SLIDE\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The bullish outlook for the rupee, coupled with a cut in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the short-term repo rate by 50 basis points to 4.5 percent on\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the weekend, saw premiums on forward dollars extend their slide\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par into a fourth consecutive day.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     The benchmark annualised six-month forward ended at 1.33\par percent, down from the previous close of 1.49 percent.\par     It has dipped 60 basis points from last week's close as\par traders priced in the repo rate cut and exporters stepped up\par encashing receivables.\par     Traders said a few banks also cut their hedged positions\par built earlier, leading to a fall in premiums.\par     Forwards reflect the outlook for the rupee, domestic money\par market liquidity and interest rate differentials between India\par and the United States.\par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                               MARKET SNAPSHOT\par                              Thursday's close   Wednesday's\par close\par     Bombay Sensitive Index (pts)     4,212.29\par 4,205.56\par     Indian rupee (/$)            45.8475/8575\par 45.84/85\par     7.27 percent 2013 bond (%)         5.2515*\par 5.2590\par     Call money (%)                  4.30/4.50\par 4.40/4.50\par     6-mth annualised fwd premium (%)     1.33\par 1.49\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030828dz8s00llu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
