\par\par \b \uc2  Indian Bonds End Higher On Low Inflation; Rupee Up Vs Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2 710 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 2003\par 14:44\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  MUMBAI -(Dow Jones)- Indian government bond prices ended higher Monday following the release of low inflation data and as the market shrugged off fears of further \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  However, a temporary mismatch in liquidity limited gains. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said sentiment in the government bond market may be turning bullish again, with recent Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  having only marginally subdued buying interest. \par \par \uc2  Further, hopes of lower interest rates have buoyed sentiment after the inflation rate slipped to 3.96% in the week ended Aug.2, its lowest level since the start of the fiscal year in April, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the benchmark 10-year bond ended at 5.53%, from 5.54% at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said that the RBI was unlikely to conduct another open market sale of bonds ahead of the sale of state government bonds scheduled for Aug. 25. The state governments of India are aiming to raise a total of INR80 billion through the sale of 12-year bonds. \par \par \uc2  Further, with liquidity expected to remain good on strong foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows, the government bond yield curve is only headed downwards, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But liquidity was a little tight Monday after recent outflows because of last week's bond auctions. The call rate ended at flat at 5.00%-5.05%. \par \par \uc2  In the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows held over a long weekend - the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was closed Friday for the independence day holiday - and bearish dollar sentiment put the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  under downward pressure. The dollar ended at INR45.855, above an intraday low of INR45.825 as state-run banks supported the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  This didn't however prevent the rupee from strengthening  to a 35-month high. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they expect the rupee to appreciate steadily to INR45.75 over the next two weeks. \par \par \uc2  As the dollar weakened in the spot market, forward dollar premiums eased. But the fall was limited as state-run banks bought short-term forward dollars. \par \par \uc2  The annualized one-year premium ended steady at 1.86% Monday. Dealers said they expect premiums to drift lower during the current week. \par \par \uc2  Indian interest swap rates ended marginally lower in line with the fall in the forward dollar premiums. \par \par \uc2  The five-year Mifor swap rate fell to 4.51%-4.56% from its previous close of 4.54%-4.59%. Mifor is the Mumbai interbank forward offered rate, which is derived from dollar forward premiums. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Rupee Vs Dollar Close\par Monday       Previous\par INR45.855    INR45.910\par Forward Dollar Premiums\par October               January                July\par INR0.250 vs INR0.255  INR0.4250 vs INR0.4275 INR0.8000 vs INR0.8125\par Daily Repurchase Auction Amount\par Monday            Previous\par INR138.25 Bln     INR140.65 Bln\par Repurchase Auction Cutoff\par Monday            Previous\par 5.0%              5.0%\par Call Rate\par Monday            Previous Close\par 5.00%-5.05%       5.00%-5.05%\par Interest Rate Swaps  Current Price   Previous Price\par  5-Year Mifor swap    4.51%-4.56%    4.54%-4.59%\par  5-Year OIS           5.12%-5.17%    5.13%-5.18%\par Benchmark Bonds\par Security   Maturity   Current Price   Previous Price\par  