\par\par \b \uc2  India's  \highlight22\uc2 fx \highlight\uc2  reserves pile to aid reforms-analysts. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Beverly Mathews \par \uc2 605 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 August 2003\par 12:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Aug 9 (Reuters) - India's surging \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, now approaching $85 billion, gives Asia's third largest economy a safety net against external shocks and provides it with muscle to push ahead with reforms, economists say. \par \par \uc2  But the world's second most populous nation must utilise these resources for growth-oriented investments because maintaining high levels of reserves is costly, they said. \par \par \uc2  India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves stood at $84.7 billion on August 1, equivalent to more than a year's imports, having risen by an average $480 million each week this year. It is just off the all time high of $84.904 billion struck a week before. \par \par \uc2  "It gives substantial room for independent policy manoeuvre and ensures that India will not really have a problem with external liquidity in the foreseeable future," said P.K. Basu, managing director at Robust Economic Analysis, Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say that high reserves mean India can continue to liberalise capital controls and should allow it to cut tariffs without risking a run on its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But there are stiff costs linked with maintaining this mountain of reserves. \par \par \uc2  For one, there is the erosive impact of the dollar's fall. Besides, bulk of the funds are invested in low-yielding U.S. assets and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has the additional task of managing the surge in domestic money market liquidity - a result of its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar purchases. \par \par \uc2  A decade of reforms has transformed \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  from a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  starved economy to one with a robust external sector position. The sluggishness in advanced economies and better local interest rates have also drawn in foreign investment flows. \par \par \uc2  "One of the big benefits of the strong external sector is that it has made foreign investors more confident," said Ajit Ranade, ABN Amro Bank's India economist. \par \par \uc2  He said while the expected buoyancy in economic growth was drawing foreign portfolio investments, an appreciating rupee backed by huge reserves was a significant confidence-booster. \par \par \uc2  Foreign funds' net investments in India stood at $2.7 billion in the first seven months of 2003 as against $744 million in the whole of the previous year, lured by attractive equity valuations and the prospects of a period of robust growth. \par \par \uc2  Portfolio investments, or 'hot money', comprise just a fifth of total reserves and analysts say that a bulk of the reserves were a result of inflows which would not reverse at short notice. \par \par \uc2  BETTER USES \par \par \uc2  But rising reserves in a capital-deficient country suggest that not enough has been done to enable resources coming from abroad to be used by sectors that need them, economists say. \par \par \uc2  The government has made little use of the reserves, pre-paying external debt worth nearly $3 billion in February. \par \par \uc2  "Sectors such as infrastructure need huge amounts of investments," said D. K. Pant, economists at the New Delhi-based National Council of Applied Economic Research. \par \par \uc2  "But our policies are still not conducive to a high level of investments because of which the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  flows entering the country are not being absorbed by productive avenues." \par \par \uc2  He said political dithering on crucial reforms such as privatisation were holding back private and foreign investments. \par \par \uc2  A high deficit was also slowing growth by diverting resources away from investments, and limiting a fall in interest rates. \par \par \uc2  But the strong external balance could help India sustain low interest rates and thus provide an incentive for investments, Basu said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say that a reduction in domestic interest rates could induce local companies to borrow more and would also discourage arbitrage-seeking foreign inflows. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030809dz8900ccq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
