\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee at fresh 33-month high despite c.bank. \b0\par\par\uc2 486 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juli 2003\par 13:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, July 14 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee\par gained in choppy trade to end at a fresh 33-month closing high\par on Monday as strong investment and trade inflows outweighed\par dollar purchases by state-run banks, which traders said were on\par behalf of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     Traders said the outlook for the rupee was bullish on\par surging overseas investments and hopes expatriate Indians will\par increase investments to benefit from relatively high local\par interest rates.\par     The rupee ended at 46.0450/0550 a dollar, off a session\par high of 46.01 but firmer than Friday's finish of 46.1050/1150\par which was the previous 33-month closing high.\par     "State-run banks capped the rupee's gains at 46.02," said a\par dealer in a government-run bank.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  "But trade remittances and\par capital flows are strong enough for the rupee to test 46 in the\par next few days."\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  through state-run banks\par in the past to check excess volatility in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market\par although it follows a policy of letting the market determine\par the rupee's exchange rate.\par     The rupee has gained more than four percent against the\par greenback so far this year despite the dollar's recent rebound\par against major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Analysts say the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may be\par averse to a sharp rupee appreciation as it would affect the\par export competitiveness of Indian firms.\par     Analysts said the rupee is now fairly valued in real,\par trade-weighted terms but was likely to rise further on capital\par inflows and remittances from expatriate Indians in the short\par run.\par     Foreign portfolio investors added a net $320.9 million\par worth of shares to their holdings in the first eight trading\par days of July after last month's net purchases for a total of\par $554 million -- the highest in two-and-a-half years.\par     Traders said news the country's largest commercial bank,\par State Bank of India, plans to raise $2 billion through a new\par deposit scheme aimed at expatriate Indians also boosted\par sentiment on the rupee. For details, please\par             .\par     Near-term premiums on the forward dollar edged up on\par sell-buy swaps by state-run banks, which traders said were on\par behalf of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Far forwards fell as exporters\par encashed their receivables on the favourable outlook for the\par rupee.\par     The three-month forward rose to an annualised 2.82 percent\par from the previous close of 2.73 percent, while the one-year\par premium ended at 2.27 percent, down from the previous close of\par 2.32 percent.\par  \par ------------------------------------------------------------\par                            MARKET SNAPSHOT\par                                  4:00 p.m. Previous close\par     Bombay Sensitive Index (pts) 3,720.75     3,676.26\par     Indian rupee (/$)            46.0450/0550 46.1050/1150\par     7.27 percent 2013 bond (%)     5.7056      5.7064*\par     Call money (%)                4.80/5.00   4.70/4.90*\par     6-mth annualised fwd premium (%) 2.36       2.35\par     * Saturday's close\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030714dz7e00mzy\par