\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee slips on c.bank dollar buying. \b0\par\par\uc2 400 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juni 2003\par 13:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, June 30 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee ended weaker\par on Monday, moving away from a near-29-month closing high struck\par last session and dropping from session highs after state-run\par banks bought dollars for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , traders said.\par     The rupee          ended at 46.4850/4925 per dollar,\par compared to Friday's close of 46.395/405, which was its highest\par close since February 6, 2001.\par     Traders said the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was dealt as high as 46.38\par in early trade before state-run banks aggressively bought the\par greenback.\par     "State-run banks bought on behalf of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  right\par through the day, and that kept dollar selling sentiment\par strongly under check," a state-run bank dealer said.\par  \par     The Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (RBI) often \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  through\par state-run banks to check market volatility.\par     Traders said Monday's purchases were more aggressive than\par normal and appeared to be aimed at holding the rupee in a range\par to help protect export competitiveness of Indian firms.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Indian exporters, including software firms, have recently\par been expressing concern over the rupee's appreciation against\par the dollar, warning it could harm their prospects.\par     Robust inflows from foreign funds and companies borrowing\par overseas at lower rates have combined to boost the rupee over\par the past 12 months.\par     The rupee gained 0.31 percent against the dollar last week\par and is up 3.22 percent in 2003. It has risen 5.58 percent from\par a life low of 49.08 hit in mid-May 2002.\par     Dealers said rupee premiums on forward dollars ended down,\par but off the day's lows, after state-run banks paid near-term\par premiums.\par     The annualised six-month premium ended three basis points\par lower at 2.75 percent, after rising off the day's trough of\par 2.67 percent.\par     Volumes in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market were lower than normal, with\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par no cash deals struck as there was no clearing of cheques due to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's annual book-keeping. India's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par follows a July-June accounting year.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  ----------------------------------------------------------\par                            MARKET SNAPSHOT\par                                Monday's closes         11:35\par a.m.\par     Bombay Sensitive Index (pts)      3,607.13\par 3,604.12\par     Indian rupee (/$)             46.4850/4925\par 46.47/49\par     9.81 percent 2013 bond (%)      not quoted\par 5.7272*\par     Call money (%)                  not quoted\par 4.75/5.00*\par     6-mth annualised fwd premium (%)      2.75\par 2.75\par     * Previous close\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030630dz6u00ody\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
