\par\par \b \uc2  India rupee extends gains, outlook bullish. \b0\par\par\uc2 362 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juni 2003\par 13:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, June 11 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee scaled a\par 26-month closing peak on Wednesday, helped by strong inflows\par from foreign funds, dollar selling by companies bringing in\par their overseas borrowings and muted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  support for\par the greenback.\par     Traders said low demand for the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  amid a\par bullish outlook for the rupee helped the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  add\par 0.20 percent, taking its total gains over the past two sessions\par to 0.39 percent.\par     The rupee          finished Wednesday at 46.7000/7050 per\par dollar, just off a high of 46.6950 and compared with Tuesday's\par close at 46.7925/8000. It last saw such levels on April 11,\par 2001, when it closed at 46.6850/6900.\par     "There were large foreign fund and ECB (external commercial\par borrowing) inflows today including from an automobile major,\par and the sheer lack of demand for the dollar and the late\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  support just took the rupee up," a foreign bank\par dealer said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "With the euro now resuming its firm undertone after a\par brief dollar bounce, the charts indicate the next target for\par dollar support at 46.35," the dealer added.\par     Traders said the Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par when it believes price movements are too sharp, bought dollars\par through state-run banks, only towards close, to support the\par 46.70 level.\par     Rupee premiums on the forward dollar however largely\par ignored the spot rupee's gains, with increased hedging of\par forward exposures by importers and corporates in the light of\par recent volatility in the spot market, lifting the near end of\par the curve.\par     The annualised premium on the benchmark six-month forward\par finished almost unchanged at 2.11 percent, off the day's low of\par 2.07 percent after corporates chose to pay the premiums. It had\par closed Tuesday at 2.12 percent.\par  \par ------------------------------------------------------------\par                                    MARKET SNAPSHOT\par                                    4:00 p.m            3:00\par p.m.\par     Bombay Sensitive Index (pts)    3,314.60*          3,320.86\par     Indian rupee (/$)           46.7000/7050*      46.7075/7175\par     9.81 percent 2013 bond (%)         5.7627            5.7612\par     Call money (%)                     closed         4.80/5.00\par     6-mth annualised fwd premium(%)     2.11*              2.11\par     * - closes\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030611dz6b00nba\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
