\par\par \b \uc2  POLL-Indian rupee to rise versus dollar to September. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Suresh Seshadri \par \uc2 544 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 2003\par 13:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, May 9 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee is expected to rise against the dollar until September and then hold steady for the rest of 2003, supported by robust trade and a strong interest rate premium over U.S. rates, a Reuters poll showed. \par \par \uc2  A poll of 20 banks, companies and research firms forecast a dollar/rupee exchange rate of 47.135 rupees at the end of June and 46.98 rupees at the end of September, compared with 47.18 rupees on Friday. \par \par \uc2  However, the poll suggested the rupee, which has weakened against the dollar for most of the past decade, would be little changed at the end of the year compared with the end of September as the dollar finds support from a pick-up in the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  "I expect the dollar to remain weak against the euro till autumn," said Saumitra Chaudhuri, economic adviser at ratings agency ICRA Ltd. "Also, exports should grow by around seven to eight percent this fiscal and capital inflows should continue." \par \par \uc2  Robust trade, capital and expatriate remittances and the dollar's global weakness pulled the rupee up from a life-low of 49.08 hit in mid-May 2002, helping it post its first annual gain in a decade. \par \par \uc2  It ended 2002 up 0.55 percent at 47.9825 and has already gained 1.7 percent so far in 2003, despite \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it will let the market determine the exchange rate but will smoothen movements in the rupee, which is only partly convertible on the capital account. \par \par \uc2  Its persistent dollar purchases in recent months have boosted the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which stood at a record high of $77.01 billion on April 25. \par \par \uc2  RATES TO BOOST INFLOWS \par \par \uc2  India's high interest rates will also continue to spur dollar inflows, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Rates have eased in recent years to multi-decade lows - the repo rate is now at five percent - but they are still way above the U.S. federal funds rate of 1.25 percent. \par \par \uc2  "There is a clear interest rate advantage and this should continue to attract flows from overseas funds and expatriates," said V Rajagopal, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  treasury at Kotak Mahindra Bank. \par \par \uc2  Indian expatriates' rupee deposits, for example, quadrupled in the ten months to January 31, 2003 to $5.04 billion. \par \par \uc2  The average of the poll's 20 responses forecast the rupee would end the year at 46.978, unchanged after September, while one-third saw it actually depreciating to an average 47.625 due to a pick-up in the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  The rupee could also face some pressure from a large outflow of about five billion dollars in repayments on Resurgent India Bonds due at the end of September, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "We expect growth signals from the U.S. economy to be far better by the fourth quarter, helping slow the dollar's depreciation," said Sanjeet Singh, analyst at ICICI Securities and Finance Company Ltd. \par \par \uc2  "So you can see the rupee's appreciation dying down towards the end of 2003." \par \par \uc2  To see related item, "TABLE-Indian rupee to rise vs dlr till Sep, flatten after", please. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030509dz5900lro\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
