\par\par \b \uc2  Indian Forward Dollar Premiums Up; Rupee At 20-Mo High \b0\par\par\uc2 571 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 2003\par 15:08\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  MUMBAI -(Dow Jones)- Dollar premiums in the forward market rose Friday, after Thursday's fall to record lows, on concerns the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be considering \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support premiums. \par \par \uc2  Market participants interpreted Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  Deputy Governor Rakesh Mohan's comment that undue \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility isn't favorable, referring to Thursday's premium fall, to mean the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may soon step in to support premiums. \par \par \uc2  Mohan also said the RBI was watching forward market developments closely, which prompted caution among traders and nudged premiums higher. \par \par \uc2  Premiums hit all-time lows Thursday as a bullish outlook for the rupee triggered heavy selling of exporters' future dollar receivables. \par \par \uc2  The annualized one-year forward dollar premium ended at 1.32% Friday, up from 1.17% Thursday. The one-year premium hit a record low of 1% Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said forward premiums are unlikely to fall much more as importers may find current low levels attractive to pay. Further, exporters' selling may decline as current levels are no longer attractive to sell. \par \par \uc2  In the spot market, the rupee extended its gains against the dollar, ending at a 20-month high of INR47.1900, compared with INR47.2400 at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the rupee's advance against the dollar mainly reflects the bearish outlook for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and expectations that India's high interest rates will continue to attract healthy \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows. \par \par \uc2  Many dealers expect the rupee to touch INR47.00 in three months' time. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, most government bonds continued to rise slowly Friday, with demand driven by plentiful money market liquidity. The ample liquidity in the system was evident from the large INR200.20 billion subscription the Reserve Bank of India received at its securities repurchase auction, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the benchmark 9.81% bond due 2013 ended at 5.91%, down from 5.92% at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  Bonds may remain in a narrow range over the next two weeks, limited on the upside by an uncertain inflation outlook but supported by the surplus liquidity. \par \par \uc2  The overnight interbank call money rate ended nearly flat at 4.75%-4.90%, compared with 4.75%-5.00% at the previous close, amid the comfortable liquidity. \par \par \uc2  In the rupee interest rate swap market, the five-year Mifor-based swap ended at 3.95%-4.05%, up slightly from the previous close of 3.90%-4.00%, in line with the higher forward premiums. Mifor is the Mumbai interbank forward offered rate, which is derived from forward-dollar premiums. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Rupee Vs Dollar Close\par Friday           Previous\par INR47.1900       INR47.2400\par Rupee Forward Premiums\par July                 October             April\par INR0.140 vs INR0.090 INR0.30 vs INR0.22  INR0.63 vs INR0.53\par Reserve Bank 6.00% Refinance\par Thursday           Previous\par INR13.9 Mln        INR13.9 Mln\par RBI Backstop Finance Facility\par Thursday           Previous\par Nil                Nil\par Daily Repurchase Auction Amount\par Friday             Previous\par INR200.20 Bln      INR217.35 Bln\par Daily Repurchase Auction Cutoff\par Friday             Previous\par 5.0%               5.0%\par Call Rate\par Friday             Previous Close\par 