\par\par \b \uc2  Iraq War Poses Risk For India's Bond Markets \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nivedita Prabhu and Manish Jaradi \par \uc2 738 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Januar 2003\par 08:14\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  MUMBAI -(Dow Jones)-  War in Iraq will send shock waves through some of India's banks and companies which stand exposed from having invested heavily in government bonds or leaving their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans unhedged. \par \par \uc2  After testing new highs over several weeks, bond prices have been thrown into reverse on fears of a war in the Gulf. The momentum increased Monday after U.N. inspectors said Iraq didn't do enough to help in the search for weapons of mass destruction. \par \par \uc2  Market players said a conflict would lead to a spike oil prices, temporary inflation and probably financial turbulence. \par \par \uc2  "Its not yet a panic situation. We are only at the first stage. The bottom may fall out later depending on how the war develops," said Amitabh Mohanty, fund manager, Alliance Capital Mutual Fund. \par \par \uc2  To ride out any shocks, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to take the necessary interest rate measures to minimize the impact on the economy of higher inflation, which will reverse a two-year downtrend in rates. \par \par \uc2  In the last two or three trading days, the government bond markets have given a sneak preview of the volatility scenarios analysts have been conjuring up against the backdrop of a potential U.S-Iraq conflict. \par \par \uc2  Since Friday, war fears have pushed bond prices off recent highs. The benchmark 10-year yield has come off from record low of 5.83% and edged back up over 6.10% \par \par \uc2  "In the next few days there will be volatility amid lower volumes," said Binai Chandgothia, head of fixed income at IDBI Principal. \par \par \uc2  Indian money markets have been bullish for nearly two years due to a low interest rate environment and low inflation expectations. \par \par \uc2  Despite United Nations inspectors' departure for Iraq two months ago, Indian markets stayed on a headlong bull run with no signs of letting up despite occasional \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI). \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the rally probably sprung from a belief that the downtrend in interest rates was irreversible - because of the massive liquidity in the system - and the RBI's often repeated stance of a softer interest rate policy and low inflation. \par \par \uc2  The rate of inflation has stayed within the RBI's forecast of 4% so far in the current financial year which ends March 31. \par \par \uc2  Over the year, bond yields have fallen over 200 basis points, most of it coming after RBI cut key interest rates late October. The bullish mood ran through the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market too, where the rupee gained nearly 2% against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    War Risks On Interest Rate Reversal\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The risk of war, however, has highlighted the vulnerability of the\par financial system as banks kept buying more and more bonds as prices kept\par rising. But such a build up of positions is vulnerable if interest rates\par were to make an about turn and rise again.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Liquidity could go the other way," said Mark Parsley, treasurer at\par Credit Agricole Indosuez, citing not only the war risk but also the\par recent easing of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  controls facilitating Indian residents\par and corporates to invest abroad, which could result in an outflow of\par dollars.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Investments by banks in bonds is over INR5 trillion compared with\par aggregate deposits of INR12.5 trillion, according to the RBI's latest\par data.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A recent study by the Indian Council of Research on International\par Economic Relations and the Indira Gandhi Institute of Development\par Research said nearly two-thirds of the top banks they sampled would\par stand to lose over 25% of their equity capital in the event of a\par 320-basis point rise in interest rates.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Companies earlier felt safe in the belief that the rupee would\par continue to appreciate, and so did not hedge large \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par positions.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Banking circles estimate that Indian companies have a total unhedged\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  position of around $7 billion to $8 billion.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Keeping unhedged positions is fine as long as the rupee appreciates\par against the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the day that changes, a scramble for dollars will trigger\par volatility which in turn could cost the RBI a few billion dollars to\par return stability to the markets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But that, like the possibility of war and the global fallout remains a\par scenario and a matter of conjecture.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Nivedita Prabhu and Manish Jaradi, Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22\par 22884212; nivedita.prabhu@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030128dz1s000en\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
