\par\par \b \uc2  Investment inflows boost Indian rupee for 3rd day. \b0\par\par\uc2 357 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Januar 2003\par 12:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Jan 8 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee appreciated against the dollar for the third straight day on Wednesday as robust direct investment inflows were met by indifferent demand for the U.S. unit. \par \par \uc2  The rupee  closed at 47.9750/47.9800 per dollar, off an intra-day high of 47.965 but up from Tuesday's 47.9775/47.9825. It has appreciated 0.05 percent since last week. \par \par \uc2  "The rupee initially strengthened on some foreign direct investment remittance and selling by some exporters, but some buying towards the end by corporates covering unhedged positions brought it off," said a trader at a state-run bank. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the lack of concerted dollar buying by state-run banks on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also helped the rupee. \par \par \uc2  "There was no visible support in the market today," a bank dealer said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  through state-run banks to support the dollar in recent weeks to protect the country's export competitiveness, traders said. \par \par \uc2  It has tried to cap the local unit's rise at around the 48 level. \par \par \uc2  The rupee rose 0.55 percent in 2002, its first annual gain in more than 10 years, helped by heavy remittances by overseas Indians, exporters and foreign investments. \par \par \uc2  In Europe, the dollar paused a cent above last week's three-year low against the euro as dealers awaited U.S. jobs data and news regarding Iraq before deciding whether to extend the greenback's recent slide. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's woes overseas have boosted remittances from non-resident Indians seeing a safe haven in the undervalued rupee and looking for better returns in view of the higher interest rates in India. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves leapt $22.25 billion last year to more than $70 billion as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  persistently absorbed inflows to rein in the rupee. \par \par \uc2  But forward dollar premium firmed after exporters holding received positions opted to pay to book profits from a narrowing spread. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark six-month premium closed at an annualised 3.48 percent against its previous close of 3.46 percent. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030108dz1800mdp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
