\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2  Jalan: RBI To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  In  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Mkt When Necessary \b0\par\par\uc2 300 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 November 2002\par 08:07\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW DELHI -(Dow Jones)- The Reserve Bank of India Governor, Bimal Jalan, said Saturday that flexibility in the exchange rate was necessary so that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had the ability to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market as and when necessary. \par \par \uc2  Jalan was speaking at a panel discussion at the meeting of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors and finance ministers of the Group of 20 nations. The Group of 20, created in 1999 to address international financial concerns, consists of the European Union and 19 separate nations: Argentina, Australia, Brazil, Canada, China, France, Germany, India, Indonesia, Italy, Japan, Mexico, Russia, Saudi Arabia, South Africa, South Korea, Turkey, the U.K. and the U.S. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of India has been mopping up dollar inflows, building up \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and preventing the rupee from sharply gaining against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Indian rupee has so far gained over 1.2% since the start of the fiscal year in April. \par \par \uc2  Jalan also said that the policy of building high level of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves should take into the anticipated current account deficits and liquidity at risk from unanticipated capital movements. \par \par \uc2  India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are at a record high of $66.024 billion with the Reserve Bank mopping up dollars sold by exporters bullish on the rupee. Dollar inflows have been rising steadily as Indians living abroad send money home to take advantage of higher local interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The Governor said a stable macro-economic environment with low inflation, low current account deficit and reasonable growth was essential to prevent crises. He said India had all three: inflation around 3%, current account deficit below 1% and growth above 5%. \par \par \uc2  -By Nivedita Prabhu, Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 22884212; nivedita.prabhu@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020021123dybn0005g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
