\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee may gain on rating upgrade prospects. \b0\par\par\uc2 314 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 November 2002\par 04:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Nov 15 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee could extend gains on Friday on prospects that Moody's Investors Services may upgrade the country's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt rating, traders said. \par \par \uc2  News of the possible review has lifted sentiment, which was earlier boosted by Iraq's decision to accept a tough U.N. resolution to disarm on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "I expect the rupee to be stronger, as an upgrade could mean increased foreign investments into the country," said a trader with a private bank. \par \par \uc2  Moody's said on Thursday it had placed India's Ba2 foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  country ceiling for debt and Ba3 foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  country ceiling for bank deposits on review for a possible upgrade. \par \par \uc2  "The review is primarily based on the substantial strengthening of the country's external financial situation," Moody's said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  The improvement in the country's external sector, showing growth in exports of goods and services, has helped the rupee to appreciate by 1.7 percent to 48.2625/2700 at Thursday's close from a life low of 49.08 hit in mid-May. \par \par \uc2  But traders were wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as state-run banks - through which the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  usually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  - have not allowed the rupee to rise above 48.25 earlier this week. \par \par \uc2  Previous closing rates: \par \par \uc2  Call money : 4.00-4.50 percent \par \par \uc2  BSE Sensex : 2,986.56 points (+0.87 percent) \par \par \uc2  Indicators: \par