\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-India's reserves drop from high on c.bank sales. \b0\par\par\uc2 534 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Mai 2002\par 11:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, May 25 (Reuters) - India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell in the week to May 17, after rising for 32 straight weeks, strengthening the market's belief that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  when war fears were strong, to steady a shaky rupee. \par \par \uc2  By May 17 - three days after an attack on an Indian army camp in Kashmir by suspected Pakistan militants jolted financial markets - the reserves fell $139 million to $55.573 billion, from the previous week's lifetime high of $55.712 billion. \par \par \uc2  The dip was mostly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets which fell $136 million to $52.433 billion, data from the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) showed. \par \par \uc2  "The drop can be attributed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It looked like it was selling dollars last week," said M.R. Madhavan, head of research at Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  The RBI, which has been buying dollars for most of the past year-and-half, absorbing strong capital inflows, reportedly turned a seller last week when war fears hit. \par \par \uc2  In the aftermath of the attack, bond prices fell around 2.5 percent and the benchmark share index slid nearly 10 percent. \par \par \uc2  But the rupee shaved off just 0.2 percent, which traders said was because of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  support. \par \par \uc2  War concerns have eased since Thursday on softer rhetoric from Indian Prime Minister Atal Behari Vajpayee and international efforts to diffuse tensions between India and Pakistan. \par \par \uc2  Vajpayee allayed fears of a full-scale war, telling a press conference in Kashmir he hoped for "clear skies" when reporters asked if he saw war clouds. \par \par \uc2  The markets are bouncing back. \par \par \uc2  The rupee has fully recovered, closing Friday at 48.9850/9925 per dollar - of its May 16 lifetime low of 49.08 - returning to levels it was at before the attack. \par \par \uc2  Tensions, however, still run high after a week of fighting across the ceasefire line in Kashmir. \par \par \uc2  RESERVES STRONG \par \par \uc2  The $139 million dip in the reserves is hardly a ripple. \par \par \uc2  But, should \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  face a more severe shock needing stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts feel the reserves will withstand it. \par \par \uc2  "If tensions worsen, the reserves may be hit," said Siddharth Mathur, strategist with JP Morgan. "The strong growth trend may not be sustained. We could even see some depletion. But, I don't think the reserves are so volatile to see a substantial outflow. \par \par \uc2  The RBI does not reveal the composition of the reserves but analysts think "hot money" may not comprise much more than a fifth of the reserves. \par \par \uc2  In financial year 2001/02 (April-March), the reserves rose by around $12 billion to touch $54.154 billion on March 29, helped by strong capital inflows. \par \par \uc2  Analysts see more growth in 2002/03 though the strength may be less. \par \par \uc2  "I expect the reserves to touch $60 billion by end March 2003 helped by a current account surplus and some direct and portfolio investments," Madhavan said. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investments in India so far in 2002 have been subdued at $622.8 million compared with the previous year's $2.8 billion. \par \par \uc2  But, analysts are hoping for a pickup as the government pushes with reforms, particularly a privatisation process. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020525d