\par\par \b \uc2  WRAP: India,Pakistan Mkts Hit As Investors Run For Cover \b0\par\par\uc2  By Saeed Azhar and Steve Percy \par \uc2 841 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Mai 2002\par 14:15\par \uc2  Dow Jones Asian Equities Report \par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY -(Dow Jones)- Markets in India and Pakistan took a direct hit Tuesday as investors ran for cover, fearing war in the troubled region. \par \par \uc2  In India's commercial capital Bombay, shares sank to a low soon after the army redeployed troops, stationed in western Gujarat state to prevent communal violence, to "areas of \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  responsibility." \par \par \uc2  The troop movement came as Indian Prime Minister Atal Bihari Vajpayee began a three-day tour of Jammu and Kashmir state to meet troops and visit an army base where alleged Pakistan-sponsored militants last week killed 34 people. \par \par \uc2  Shelling continued on both sides of the so-called Line of Control that separates Indian- and Pakistani-administered Kashmir. \par \par \uc2  Equity markets in both countries were hit on heightened tensions. The Bombay Stock Exchange Sensitive Index, or Sensex, closed down 2.9% at 3186.53 - its lowest level this calendar year - but off an intraday low of 3127.87. The index finished at a low of 3184.44 Dec. 28. \par \par \uc2  Across the border, the Karachi Stock Exchange 100-share index ended down 3% at 1598.16 points - its lowest level since January - in spite of regulatory measures imposed to stem a sharp fall. \par \par \uc2  The mood on the Bombay bourses was glum, as traders crowded around to watch live television coverage of the shelling along the border. \par \par \uc2  "There's panic all round, but there are technical reasons too, for today's (Tuesday's) sharp fall," said Kisan Ratilal Choksey, chairman of KRC Shares & Securities. \par \par \uc2  "Most stocks are fundamentally strong and there's no specific reason to exit them. But most mutual funds or institutional investors don't like these certainties and are selling," Choksey said. \par \par \uc2  Shares across the board suffered, including those of petrochemicals concern Reliance Industries Ltd. (P.REL) and Hindustan Lever Ltd. (P.HLV), India's largest conglomerate. Reliance Industries fell 4.7% to 262.40 rupees ($1=INR48.9850), while Hindustan Lever lost 1.2% to INR197.25. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Regulator Fails To Curb Karachi Losses\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In Pakistan, emergency measures failed to prop up stocks on the\par Karachi Stock Exchange as retail investors heavily sold their\par shareholdings.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par KSE authorities tightened the bourse's circuit-breakers after the\par KSE-100 share index plunged 7.4% Monday, in its biggest one-day loss\par since October 1997.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Shares will now be allowed a maximum daily fall of 5%, instead of\par 7.5%. They will continue to be allowed to rise a maximum of 7.5% daily.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Selling, triggered by the border tensions, intensified on settlement\par fears, said Mohammad Sohail, research head at Investcap Securities.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I expect some recovery Wednesday as institutions will be supportive\par and retail investors will cover short-positions," Sohail said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Others, however, were less certain.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In Pakistan's bond markets, no bids were reported for three- and\par five-year government bonds ahead of their auction Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The bond market is as dull as you can get. Yesterday (Monday), we had\par some bids, but today (Tuesday) players have disappeared," a dealer in\par Karachi said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market had a brief respite on the absence of\par demand from importers. Also, inflows from exporters allowed the rupee to\par rise to PKR59.95 to the dollar, breaching the critical PKR60.00 barrier\par for the first time since March.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The rupee's gains came in spite of dollar buying by Pakistan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We will not allow any speculator to play with the (Pakistan) rupee\par due to tensions with India. We won't allow what happened to the stock\par market yesterday (Monday)," Zafar Sheikh, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  adviser of\par the State Bank of Pakistan, told Dow Jones Newswires.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Indian Bonds Fall As Rupee Gains\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In Bombay, government bonds fell in thin trade due to uncertainties\par caused by rising tensions with Pakistan.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The 10-year bond touched 8.22%, up from the previous close of 8.14%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par However, selling was limited as traders avoided the markets, dealers\par said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In contrast, the Indian rupee ended higher at INR48.985 to the dollar,\par against Monday's close of INR49.030, due to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par through state-owned banks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  probably didn't want the rupee and the\par Sensex to fall sharply on the same day, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "(The RBI) feels there isn't enough customer demand. The rupee\par shouldn't weaken on speculation - that is the RBI's message," said K.\par Jayaram, chief dealer at Credit Lyonnais Bank in Bombay.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The threat of conflict was evident in the forward market. Rupee\par premiums on forward dollar and Overnight Indexed Swap rates climbed.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par April premiums closed at 2.80 against the previous close of 2.75,\par while one-year Overnight Indexed Swap rates ended sharply higher at\par 7.46%-7.50%, against the previous close of 7.30-7.34%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par - By Saeed Azhar and Steve Percy, Dow Jones Newswires; 91-22-288-4212;\par djn.in@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Nivedita Prabhu, Sophia Rodrigues and Ashok Bhattacharjee in Bombay\par contributed to this story)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument aer0000020020521dy5l005pl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
