\par\par \b \uc2  India rupee set for cool stint,some cbank-steering. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Vidya Ranganathan \par \uc2 732 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 April 2002\par 11:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, April 10 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee, driven to new lows this week, will keep its softening bias over the next 12 months though its decline should be cushioned by improving global growth and strong foreign capital inflows. \par \par \uc2  Economists polled by Reuters expected the rupee to continue weakening in keeping with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's perceived preference for an export-supportive exchange rate, but its fall should be in line with inflation differences with the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The risks to that firm view could come from a sharper-than-expected spike in international oil prices, a sudden devaluation of the Chinese yuan or domestic political turmoil. \par \par \uc2  "Barring the unforeseen event that oil prices go up significantly, the rupee will remain very strong," said Sanjay Mathur, economist with UBS Warburg, Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "Exports would start to perform this year, inflation is low and the external account is strong," he added. The poll forecast the rupee at 49.66 per dollar by the end of September and 50.54 at the close of 2002/03 (April-March), a drop of 3.2 percent during the year from current levels of 48.90/91. \par \par \uc2  That compares with a four percent average annual fall in the past decade since partial trade account convertibility was introduced, and a five-year average depreciation of 5.3 percent. \par \par \uc2  Economists said their forecasts factored in the likely risks of further tensions in the Middle East or a threat to India's multi-party government from ongoing Hindu-Muslim clashes, re-ignited last month by the dispute over a religious site. \par \par \uc2  "The premium on these event risks has been added to the trend rate of depreciation," said Ajit Ranade, ABN AMRO Bank economist. \par \par \uc2  "If the Hindu-Muslim conflict flares up, all the government's resources and management process will be diverted from reforms." The partly convertible rupee lost 4.5 percent in the previous year and struck an all-time low of 48.96 on Tuesday. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  Last week the rupee was tripped up by fears the government would use its huge $54 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to retire external debt in advance, and rocked by rising oil prices. \par \par \uc2  Government borrowings account for a large chunk of the $100 billion external debt, of which funds raised through placement of bonds with expatriates in 1998 and 2000 account for a tenth. \par \par \uc2  Bankers say the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (RBI) aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent months was to accumulate funds for repaying the first set of those bonds in 2003 while also keeping the rupee fairly valued in trade-weighted terms. \par \par \uc2  The RBI added about $3.4 billion to reserves in March alone. \par \par \uc2  And, its constant presence in the regulated market was an important factor in all calculations, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "As an economist, I expect that the rupee should depreciate about 2.5 percent to restore real parity, based on the difference in consumer prices inflation," said Sanjeet Singh, analyst with ICICI Securities and Finance Corp (I-Sec). \par \par \uc2  "To that one has to add the inelastic dollar demand from the RBI, because of its conscious strategy of building up reserves." \par \par \uc2  IMPORT BARRIER \par \par \uc2  A key reason the RBI would want to keep the rupee on a downward gradient was the need to boost exports and counter the likely onslaught of imports as tariffs drop, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "There would be some tendency to use the rupee to protect domestic industry from imports and push exports, a transition from old methods like tariffs and export subsidies," said Ranade. \par \par \uc2  Still, despite the cyclical upturn in the economy, dealers said there was no threat of an increase in imports. \par \par \uc2  "There could be a consumption boom, but an investment boom is unlikely. There is no capacity constraint yet," said Sanjeev Sanyal, regional economist with Deutsche Bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  The RBI estimates the current account will be in surplus for the past year ended in March, for the first time in 25 years, helped by shrinking imports and nearly flat export growth. \par \par \uc2  Economists say current account balances are in any case small and any deficit next year will easily be soaked up by sustained capital inflows from portfolio and direct foreign investments. \par \par \uc2  They expect fresh investments will flow into labour-intensive exports, drawn by incentives in the special export-focused economic zones announced in the government's new trade policy. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020410dy4a00j54\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
