\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 India forex \highlight\uc2  reserves at new high, \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  seen. \b0\par\par\uc2 426 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Februar 2002\par 09:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Feb 16 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have touched a new high for the 20th straight week, which analysts said showed the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the rupee down. \par \par \uc2  On February 8 the reserves stood at $49.766 billion, up $201 million from the previous week, the RBI said in a weekly statistical supplement. \par \par \uc2  Traders and analysts said the reserves were bolstered by trade and investment related inflows which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had absorbed in the face of weak importer dollar demand, and the $50 billion mark now beckoned. \par \par \uc2  "I think there was an overt \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to drive the rupee slightly lower during the week," said Uday Mulgaonkar, associate vice president at Kotak Mahindra Capital Company. \par \par \uc2  "A correction was justified as an overvalued rupee would hurt India's export competitiveness in the sluggish global economic conditions," he added. \par \par \uc2  The rupee closed Friday at 48.6650/6750, still overvalued by over a percent, according to analysts. On February 8 it had closed at 48.7000/7100. \par \par \uc2  After a period of relative stability in the last quarter of 2001, the rupee has depreciated by around two percent since late December while the reserves have risen by around $1.67 billion. \par \par \uc2  Traders and analysts said this indicated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's hand to keep the rupee fairly valued on a traded-weighted basis as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of some of India's largest trading partners, including Europe and Japan, have weakened. \par \par \uc2  Left to market forces, traders said the rupee would have traded much above its fair value because of robust investment inflows and falling import payments due to sluggish domestic industrial activity and softer oil prices. \par \par \uc2  MORE RISES IN STORE \par \par \uc2  Over the past year, India has amassed a substantial $8 billion in reserves, but traders foresaw more rises. \par \par \uc2  They expect the reserves to top the $50 billion mark by the end of the month as investment related inflows remained strong. \par \par \uc2  Portfolio inflows have picked up in February since the government successfully sold its stake in some leading firms. \par \par \uc2  Between February 1-14, their net foreign fund investments in the secondary market totalled $201.2 million compared with $137.4 million through January. \par \par \uc2  And they are expected to pump in more funds with the stock market looking forward to an expected rally ahead of the 2002/03 (April-March) federal budget announcement on February 28. \par \par \uc2  Foreign direct investments have also come in, as have inflows from foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans in recent sessions, traders said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020216dy2g00cch\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
