\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-India's  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  reserves fall for second week. \b0\par\par\uc2 416 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2001\par 11:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Sept 29 (Reuters) - India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell for a second straight week which analysts attributed to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the wobbly rupee. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) on Saturday said the reserves were down $584 million at $44.704 billion on September 21, from $45.288 billion the previous week. The fall was entirely due to a decline in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets. \par \par \uc2  The rupee plunged to record lows that week with the market, gripped in the storm that engulfed global and domestic financial markets in the aftermath of the September 11 attacks on the United States. \par \par \uc2  On September 17, the rupee fell to its lifetime low of 48.43 per dollar, down 2.7 percent from September 1 and 3.7 percent from the beginning of the year. \par \par \uc2  "It was heavy RBI dollar sales that week and the week before that was key in stemming the slide and prompting a partial recovery (in the rupee)," an analyst said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee ended September 21 at a lifetime closing low of 48.01/03 per dollar before clawing its way back to close on Friday at 47.865/8700 per dolllar. During the week ended September 21, major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also fell against the dollar with the euro and yen depreciating by around 0.6 percent and the British pound by around one percent. \par \par \uc2  Part of India's reserves are denominated in these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The reserves are down from an all-time high of $45.365 billion recorded on September 7. \par \par \uc2  RESERVES COMFORTABLE TO SUPPORT RUPEE \par \par \uc2  Traders said India's reserves were more than adequate to hold off any undue attack on the rupee which, in any case, is unlikely to come under pressure unless war tensions escalated. \par \par \uc2  "So, I don't see any more pressures on the reserves at least not for a while," an analyst said. "The rupee's trend is weaker. But there is not cause for any sharp tumble." \par \par \uc2  At its Friday close, the rupee was fairly valued on a trade-weighted basis, analysts said. Looking ahead, they said importer dollar demand was expected to remain dull due to a global industrial slowdown and the softening of oil prices. \par \par \uc2  But they added there are concerns about slowing portfolio investments with foreign funds turning net sellers of Indian assets in September. \par \par \uc2  These funds' net sales were $91.2 million during September 3-27, after being net buyers from January through August. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010929dx9t00c1d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
