\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-India rupee breathes easy, shares in relief rally. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Giri Venkatesan \par \uc2 591 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2001\par 14:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Sept 18 (Reuters) - India's battered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  got some respite on Tuesday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  soothed sentiment, while shares saw a relief recovery after Wall Street performed within expectations on reopening for trade. \par \par \uc2  But traders said sentiment remained cautious, given that the domestic economy was grappling with a two-year slowdown and there were growing concerns of a global recession. \par \par \uc2  Fears over the possible fallout from expected U.S. reprisals against Afghanistan, which is sheltering Osama bin Laden, the prime suspect in last week's attacks on the United States, also weighed on the market. \par \par \uc2  The rupee hit a new closing low of 47.97/99 per dollar, dropping below its previous closing low of 47.83/88 hit on Monday, but well off its lifetime trough of 48.43, struck on the same day. It has now hit new closing lows for eight days in a row. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark Bombay exchange index snapped a nine-day losing streak to end up 3.79 percent at 2,782.47 points, off 2,814.07. \par \par \uc2  At its close on Monday, the index had lost 15 percent since the attacks on the U.S.. In comparison, the Dow Jones index fell only 7.13 percent on Monday, when it resumed after a four-day shut down. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  SUPPORT \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and debt markets were heartened by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) has offered to purchase four bonds on Tuesday to provide market liquidity. \par \par \uc2  On Monday it was believed to have sold dollars through state-run banks after the rupee hit record lows for the seventh straight session. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said state-run banks sold dollars on Tuesday also, boosting the rupee to an intra-day high of 47.85 before it eased on demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from private and foreign banks. \par \par \uc2  "The event risk (of a war in the region) is there, forcing banks to buy dollars," a dealer in a private bank said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said trade was much less volatile than on Monday when the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell 0.61 rupees or 1.28 percent. \par \par \uc2  At Tuesday's close, the rupee had lost 2.77 percent against the dollar, compared with an average annual depreciation of five to six percent in recent years. \par \par \uc2  Dealers suspect its latest spell of weakness, which started before the attacks in the U.S., was initially with the RBI's blessings and meant to correct an overvaluation in trade-weighted terms against major trading partners. \par \par \uc2  It is now estimated to be undervalued by over one percent. \par \par \uc2  While the immediate impact of current global developments on India's relatively closed economy are still unclear, analysts fear they will delay hopes of a recovery from a two-year slowdown. \par \par \uc2  They are worried over a possible slowdown in foreign investments and a hike in global crude oil prices if tensions flare up. India imports more than two-thirds of its crude oil requirements. \par \par \uc2  SHARES FIRM ON REGIONALS \par \par \uc2  Share prices took comfort from firm regional markets. \par \par \uc2  "The overnight Fed rate cut and firm strong regionals have held the market," said Chetan Kapoor at KJMC Capital Markets. \par \par \uc2  Advances outnumbered losers 670 to 308 on the Bombay exchange but traders said underlying sentiment was shaky and investors were unwilling to take on bigger positions. \par \par \uc2  "A rebound was expected after the sharp decline but investors are yet to be convinced about a sustained rally," said Prabhakar Rao, a fund manager with UTI Bank. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010918dx9i00o2s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
