\par\par \b \uc2  Indian Rupee Rescued By  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  As US Fears Mount \b0\par\par\uc2  By S. Anuradha \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 633 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2001\par 17:37\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY -(Dow Jones)- Three major state-owned banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Monday to support the rupee, which is suffering from fears India could get caught up in any conflict involving Afghanistan and the U.S. \par \par \uc2  Bankers told Dow Jones Newswires the moves could bring respite to the rupee, though this may only prove temporary. \par \par \uc2  The banks concerned, acting on behalf of the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  - which doesn't directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market - aggressively sold dollars, helping the rupee to end at INR47.76, sharply up from its all-time traded low of INR48.40. The rupee closed Friday at INR47.83, its seventh consecutive closing low. \par \par \uc2  "It was good the RBI \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the market was turning very volatile," said Jayesh Doshi, general manager (treasury) at Gujarat Ambuja Cements Ltd. \par \par \uc2  The American government's prime suspect in last week's airborne attacks on New York and Washington D.C., Osama bin Laden, lives in Afghanistan. \par \par \uc2  A fall in the local stock market also damped rupee sentiment as there are fears that foreign funds selling Indian shares would step up dollar purchases as they send the proceeds back home. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark Bombay Stock Exchange Sensitive Index or Sensex ended down 149.14 points, or 5.3%, at 2680.98 Monday, an eight-year low. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank's moves came after its statement on Saturday that it would sell dollars directly or indirectly to ease any demand-supply gaps in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also said with \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of over $45.00 billion it could easily provide liquidity in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Bankers said that by choosing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the dollar touched a high of INR48.40, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was able to drive home its message. \par \par \uc2  "The market was already at thin volume at INR47.40 and the dollar sales forced banks to liquidate their long dollars in a hurry," said a dealer at a large European bank. \par \par \uc2  Monday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was estimated at between $100-$200 million. \par \par \uc2  Nerves Will Persist \par \par \uc2  Piyush Kaul, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Hongkong & Shanghai Banking Corp. said he expects the rupee to trade in a narrower intraday band Tuesday. "I am looking at a INR47.75-48.10 range tomorrow (Tuesday)," he added. \par \par \uc2  However, the current political flux resulting from the U.S outrage may yet prevent the rupee from regaining a stable footing, though some bankers added that INR48.50 may be the low for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the short term. \par \par \uc2  Kaul said developments in the South Asian region and the recent grim statements on the economy from the Prime Minister will continue to keep the market long on the dollar rather than short. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister A.B Vajpayee, in a speech to the nation Friday, said exports from India and foreign direct investment into the country may slow down in the aftermath of the tragedy, while even a small increase in global oil prices could push up the country's oil import bill sharply. \par \par \uc2  Bankers also said the Reserve Bank may not be eager to sell dollars in the market every day if it fears that foreign capital inflows may be lower in the months to come. \par \par \uc2  Foreign funds have already net sold shares and bonds worth $39.7 million so far in September - the first month when they turned net sellers - and there are fears that they may sell more if they face redemption pressures back home in the U.S. The recent sell-off in the Indian stock markets has been attributed to share sales by U.S-based foreign funds. \par \par \uc2  -By S. Anuradha, Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 2884211; su.anuradha@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010917dx9h02j5k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
