\par\par \b \uc2  India RBI View May Soothe Mkts, But Event Risks Remain \b0\par\par\uc2  By S. Anuradha \par \uc2 739 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2001\par 14:42\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY -(Dow Jones)- The Reserve Bank of India's Saturday statement that it won't change current rate policy and will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect the rupee will soothe Indian bond and rupee markets. However, statement unlikely to have lasting impact given the uncertainty over global events after the terrorist attacks in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  "RBI statement will do more good than bad," an analyst at foreign broking house said. \par \par \uc2  On the other hand, the head of dealing at a U.S bank said, "RBI's statement may bring stability in the market, but there is still too much event risk. No one has seen a scenario like this before." \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of India said Saturday there wouldn't be any change in its interest-rate policy and that it will sell dollars to protect the rupee. Reserve Bank also said that with record high \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of $45.00 billion it could ensure liquidity in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Indian bond and financial markets have weakened sharply on fears that the country may also be drawn into a conflict possibly involving the U.S. and Afghanistan, home of prime suspect Osama bin Laden. The Indian government has already offered the use of its facilities for any U.S. military \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  against terrorists targets, including air strikes on Afghanistan. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the rupee closed to a record of INR47.83 against previous close INR47.57. This was the sixth consecutive day the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to a new low. Bonds prices have also crashed with benchmark ten-year 11.50% 2011 bond falling to a low of 110 on Saturday from 115.49 on Tuesday when the terrorist attacks took place. 11.50% 2011 closed at 111.50 Saturday. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank's comments Saturday come after spending, according to market sources, anywhere between $200 million-$400 million since Wednesday to curb the rupee's fall in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. In the money market, it also has reportedly persuaded the State Bank of India (P.SBI), the country's largest bank to lend interbank call money at 7.00% Friday much lower than the then prevailing 8.00%. RBI's desired to keep call rates stable because a rising rate would cause banks to sell bonds more aggressively. \par \par \uc2  Market Awaits RBI Dlr Sales \par \par \uc2  But the market waking up to bad news everyday wants more. \par \par \uc2  Siddharth Mathur, analyst at JP Morgan Securities Ltd. said Reserve Bank's statement are a little less that what market expected. "Market wants to see at what level RBI will start aggressively supporting the rupee." \par \par \uc2  Not surprising then that market is waiting for Monday to see whether state-owned banks will start selling dollars on behalf of the Reserve Bank. \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  indirectly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market through a couple of state-owned banks. \par \par \uc2  "If RBI doesn't ensure a stable or a slightly lower opening for the rupee it could easily slip to INR48-48.20," the U.S bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers, though, remain divided over whether aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is required from Reserve Bank to help the rupee. Some dealers said that disruption of banking activities in New York has held up dollar supplies and could ease pressure on the rupee when they find their way into the market. \par \par \uc2  On the other hand, the treasurer at a U.K. Bank said rupee is unlikely to recover lost ground on its own. "In a volatile market rupee never settles down on its own. It needs RBI's help." \par \par \uc2  The bond market also thinks it could do with some active \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Reserve Bank although some its problems will be solved if some sanity is restored to the rupee market. \par \par \uc2  The treasurer at the U.K. bank said the market is waiting for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to provide a rate signal by purchasing bonds from the market through its open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Some dealers though think Reserve Bank won't buy bonds aggressively because the increased liquidity in the system will add to speculation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  A market source also said it was unlikely Reserve Bank will buy bonds from the market. "RBI has two options either sell dollars or tinker with the repurchase agreement rate," he added. \par \par \uc2  -By S. Anuradha, Dow Jones Newswires; 9122-2884211;su.anuradha@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010915dx9f0007e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
