\par\par \b \uc2  Will flows help Indian rupee escape August blues? \b0\par\par\uc2  By Vidya Ranganathan \par \uc2 751 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 August 2001\par 12:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Aug 9 (Reuters) - Strong foreign investment inflows seem to be helping the Indian rupee ride out what has traditionally been the most tumultuous month of the year, but traders are still keeping their fingers crossed. \par \par \uc2  The rupee has fallen an average five to six percent annually in the past six years, with some of the sharpest drops taking place in August. \par \par \uc2  It has not reacted much so far to bad news this month. \par \par \uc2  International rating agencies have downgraded their outlook on the country, foreign portfolio inflows have slowed to a trickle and the federal coalition government is fending off accusations over a crisis at the largest mutual fund manager. \par \par \uc2  Standard & Poor's downgraded India's local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating and outlook on Tuesday and was followed by rival rating agency Moody's Investors Services revising its outlook the next day. \par \par \uc2  That's against the backdrop of sharply slower economic growth, a near recession in industry and no signs that India can trim its burgeoning government fiscal deficit. \par \par \uc2  The rupee however seems loath to react. \par \par \uc2  In Thursday afternoon deals, it was quoted at 47.14/15 to a dollar, down less than a percent since the start of 2001. It has stayed in a steady 47.10-47.16 range since early July. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the slowdown in industrial and capital imports coupled with inflows from overseas equity raised by some sectors was supporting the rupee. \par \par \uc2  "There's definitely a recession, demand for dollars has been muted. At the same time, inflows have been positive," said V. Ravikumar, head of private sector Vysya Bank's treasury. \par \par \uc2  Others felt it was \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , through state-owned banks, and Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) Governor Bimal Jalan's assurances on an easier monetary policy that were helping the rupee check losses this week. \par \par \uc2  Some traders said the current stability was only a postponement of an inevitable downmove. \par \par \uc2  "The immediate short-term fortunes of the rupee are driven by actual flows, so it is a very difficult call to take," said S.Varadarajan, dealer at Chase Manhattan. \par \par \uc2  "But foreign flows are turning negative, that's worrying." \par \par \uc2  FICKLE FOREIGN FLOWS \par \par \uc2  So far, foreign direct and equity investment has been robust, and has boosted foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to record highs over $43 billion. \par \par \uc2  Several firms including telecommunications giant Bharti Enterprises and private sector HDFC Bank have raised money from foreign investors. \par \par \uc2  The country has just obtained half a billion dollars worth of preferential trade access with the United States, which could boost flagging exports. \par \par \uc2  But export growth in the April-June quarter was just 1.76 percent compared with the 20 percent jump in 2000/01. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the downgrades will not affect sector-specific equity investments, yet India's credibility with foreign investors is under a cloud after a bitter dispute between energy giant Enron Corp and local utility Maharashtra State Electricity Board over high tariffs and payment defaults. \par \par \uc2  Enron is keen to exit the $2.9 billion project, the largest single direct investment in the country. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investments in stocks and bonds have been heavy, despite the host of scandals the stock market has suffered since March and the 17 percent drop in the benchmark Bombay index so far in 2001, but have ebbed of late. \par \par \uc2  Foreign portfolio inflows so far in 2001 exceed $2.6 billion compared with $1.56 billion through the whole of 2000, but in July they were just $91 billion \par \par \uc2  THE "AUGUST" FACTOR \par \par \uc2  AUGUST 1995 - Heavy oil imports and media reports that the government will start routing foreign debt repayment through the market pressured the rupee which had been stable in a 31.30-31.40 band for nearly 18 months. It hit 33.90 in September 1995. \par \par \uc2  AUGUST 1997 - The rupee slipped to 36.50 after a year of trading 35.70-35.85. Several factors played a role, including reports that the government was contemplating a five percent trading band and Reserve Bank of India (RBI) Deputy Governor Y.V. Reddy's remarks that the rupee was overvalued in real terms. \par \par \uc2  AUGUST 1998 - It fell from around 42.50 to a low of 46.27, recovered and resumed the decline in later months. The reasons were weak regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and heavy import demand. \par \par \uc2  AUGUST 2000 - Decade-high global oil prices raised concerns about India's trade account. The rupee fluctuated between a high of 45.02 and a low of 46.08 in August 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010809dx8900byo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
