\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee ends weak, traders see technical dip. \b0\par\par\uc2 240 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 2001\par 13:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, June 12 (Reuters) - The rupee ended weaker on import demand on Tuesday with traders interpreting the dip as the first move in a technical downward correction. \par \par \uc2  The rupee ended at 47.0150/0200, off 47.0350 and compared to Monday's 46.98/99. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said state-run banks bought dollars, believed to be on behalf of an oil firm, while another private sector petrochemicals firm also bought. \par \par \uc2  "The close above 47 is significant. State-run banks also bought dollars, so maybe we are in for some technical correction in the rupee," the chief trader at a private bank said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee firmed to levels above 47 in early June, recovering a brief bout of weakness after international rating agency Fitch revised India's sovereign ratings outlook to negative from stable, and was supported by foreign equity inflows and portfolio investments. \par \par \uc2  The market had shrugged off the rupee's current four percent overvaluation against its trade-weighted index, following the weakness in European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Japanese yen. \par \par \uc2  But this week's dollar purchases by state-run firms and banks has raised doubts the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be trying to gradually adjust the rupee's value downward to bring it closer to its trade-weighted real effective exchange rate (REER). \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) normally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  through large state-run banks. \par \par \uc2  For a speed guide to Reuters treasury coverage, \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx6c01wr4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pic