\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee ends firmer, forwards close off highs. \b0\par\par\uc2 296 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 2001\par 13:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, May 16 (Reuters) - The rupee ended Wednesday higher, snapping a four-day losing streak, on steady dollar inflows from foreign funds and exporters, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee ended at 46.9350/9450 per dollar, firmer than Tuesday's close of 46.9800/00 but slightly off intra-day highs of 46.9250. \par \par \uc2  The rupee had lost nearly 0.28 percent since the weekend to touch an intra-day low of 47.04 in some deals on Tuesday on dollar purchases from a large oil firm. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said rupee's gains on Wednesday were capped by from some large state-run banks, apparently acting on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) often \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  through state-run banks to curb volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said banks offering custodial services to foreign portfolio investors sold large lots of dollars on Wednesday morning, reflecting growing investments by foreign funds in India. \par \par \uc2  Data from the capital market regulator shows that foreign funds invested a net $102.4 million in Indian bonds and stocks this month till May 14, taking their total investments this year to $2.22 billion compared to $1.56 billion in the whole of 2000. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said demand from importers was thin on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Forward dollar premiums eased after opening firm, on growing expectations that the RBI may follow the U.S. Fed Reserve's example and cut the bank rate from the current seven percent soon. \par \par \uc2  Dealers had expected forwards to firm to reflect growing interest rate differentials between the U.S and India. \par \par \uc2  The six-month forward premium ended Wednesday at 4.97 percent annualised compared to Tuesday's 4.98 percent. \par \par \uc2  For a speed guide to Reuters treasury coverage, \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx5g03qog\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
