\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee slightly weaker, seen under pressure. \b0\par\par\uc2 313 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 2000\par 05:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Nov 28 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee was marginally weaker little changed in early Tuesday deals but the undertone on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was weak on steady dollar bids, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  At 9:20 a.m. (0350 GMT), the rupee was quoted at 46.88/89 per dollar, compared to its previous close of 46.875/88. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said early trade was thin, with state-run banks quoting in line with the market. State-run banks are often seen as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  surrogates and are closely watched for clues to the rupee's direction. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the longer term outlook for the rupee remained bearish, given the dollar's strength against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The rupee now appears to be be over-valued on its real effective exchange rate by around two percent," said a dealer at a foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  "So we should see some more weakness in the rupee," he said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said other factors exerting pressure on the rupee were firm global oil prices and sluggish foreign fund inflows. \par \par \uc2  "Overall, there are no positives for the rupee at the moment," said a dealer at a state-run bank. "There should be a gradual slide in the coming days." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they expected the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , mainly through state-run banks, only if there was sharp volatility in the market. \par \par \uc2  They said the RBI was in a position to tackle any unexpected pressure on the rupee, thanks to a recent sharp jump in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. \par \par \uc2  In the two weeks to November 17, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose by $2.34 billion to $37.077 billion, crossing $37 billion for the first time since June. \par \par \uc2  Dealers attributed the rises to inflows from an overseas deposit offering by the State Bank of India, which closed on November 6 after raising $5.5 billion. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwbs00d5y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
