\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee hits new low as dollar supplies dry up. \b0\par\par\uc2 629 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2000\par 10:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Oct 27 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee hit new lows against the dollar on Friday as corporates stepped up dollar purchases amid concerns over firm world oil prices and weak regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  They said demand was sparked by the absence of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite the rupee breaching psychological barriers but stressed that volumes were low. \par \par \uc2  In afternoon deals, the rupee was quoted at 46.74/75 per dollar, slightly off its lifetime intra-day low of 46.765. \par \par \uc2  At its new low the rupee, convertible only on the current account, was nearly seven percent weaker than at the start of the calendar year. \par \par \uc2  Traders, however, expect some respite for the rupee next week. \par \par \uc2  "Corporates are covering their requirements in a thin market, but I think the rupee should stabilise in a broad 46.75/90 level," Surendra Rosha, head of treasury sales at HSBC, said. \par \par \uc2  "I expect better dollar supplies next week as these are extremely attractive rates for exporters to sell," he said. \par \par \uc2  However longer term concerns have not yet been cleared and the undertone on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would remain bearish, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee has been under pressure since May this year as foreign capital inflows slowed while global crude oil prices remained firm. \par \par \uc2  Foreign funds has so far invested a net of $282.5 million in the current financial year, compared with $2.34 billion in 1999/00 (April-March). \par \par \uc2  While inflows from foreign funds have slowed to a trickle, outflows on account of oil have risen. \par \par \uc2  India imports a large part of its crude oil requirements and its import bill for 2000/01 (April-March) is expected to jump to $17.5 billion from $12.3 billion a year earlier if global prices remain at current levels. \par \par \uc2  Analysts expect India's balance of payments position to be square in 2000/01 after four years of surpluses. India had a $1.021 billion deficit in its balance of payments in the first quarter of 2000/01. \par \par \uc2  "Periodical upheavals in the capital market might drive the rupee lower in the near term and I do not expect it to recoup any of its earlier losses as longer term concerns are still there," K.S Harshan, head of international banking at ICICI Bank said. \par \par \uc2  EURO, STERLING FALLS NOT HELPING THE RUPEE \par \par \uc2  A weak euro and sterling were also adding to pressure on the rupee, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee has fallen nearly one percent this week, with no signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI). \par \par \uc2  The RBI has said that the rupee's value is market-determined and that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has no target for its value. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's past actions indicated that it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  only if there was excessive volatility or indications that trade was speculative. \par \par \uc2  "With the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  allowing the rupee to find its own levels, my guess is the rupee should weaken further gradually," a dealer in a foreign bank said. \par \par \uc2  He said the rupee was still overvalued by nearly two percentage points on trade-weighted terms due to a recent jump in the domestic inflation rate and the weakness of the euro and sterling. \par \par \uc2  MILD BOOST SEEN FROM OVERSEAS DEPOSIT SCHEME \par \par \uc2  Sentiment for the rupee might get a mild boost in the short term on inflows from an ongoing overseas deposit scheme launched by the State Bank of India (SBI), dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The SBI's India Millennium Deposit scheme, which was launched on Saturday, has so far collected $1 billion, a senior SBI official said. \par \par \uc2  Inflows through the scheme will boost India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves which are down nearly eight percent from their mid-April peak of $38.34 billion. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwar03arm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
