\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-Indian rupee at new low, seen weakening further. \b0\par\par\uc2 637 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 August 2000\par 15:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Aug 8 (Reuters) - Persistent dollar demand from importers drove the Indian rupee to fresh lows against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday and analysts expected it to weaken further. \par \par \uc2  There was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) is depending on a progressive tightening of money market liquidity to drive short-term interest rates higher to curb any excessive rupee selling. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the one-day government securities repurchase agreements (repos) was set at 14.0 percent on Tuesday from 8.25 percent last Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The rupee ended at a new low of 45.73/74 per dollar after being dealt at an intra-day low of 45.745. It closed at 45.55/56 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The rupee has now lost 0.7 percent in the last two trading sessions and is 4.9 percent lower against the dollar than at the start of the year, although it has held its ground or appreciated against most other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Analysts said there had been little reaction to Monday's announcement by Singapore Telecommunications that it planned to invest $400 million in two Indian telecoms firms, but news of more foreign direct investment could bolster the rupee. \par \par \uc2  Technical analysts said, however, that the charts pointed to further weakness for the rupee. \par \par \uc2  Tom Schroeder, head of global technical analysis at SG Securities said he saw the rupee dropping to 46 per dollar in the next two or three trading sessions. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the weaker rupee reflected India's widening trade deficit, a slowdown in foreign capital inflows and the dollar's recent strength against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers and analysts did not expect any fast turnaround in those fundamentals and expected the rupee to ease further. \par \par \uc2  "If the current trend against the rupee has to reverse, it could only be with aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a dealer at an European bank. \par \par \uc2  But that seems a limited option. The RBI has run down its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves by more than $2.0 billion from their mid-April peak of $38.341 billion. \par \par \uc2  Analysts are doubtful about the effectiveness of the RBI's liquidity tightening measures as demand for dollars appears to be genuine importer demand, which is outweighing dollar receipts from exporters and foreign investors. \par \par \uc2  The next phase of the RBI's rupee support package, announced on July 21, kicks in on Saturday when banks' cash reserve ratios (CRR) will be raised from 8.25 percent to 8.5 percent and their refinancing limits will be cut further. \par \par \uc2  FOREIGN FUND SELLING ABATES \par \par \uc2  India's trade deficit - most of India's trade is denominated in dollars - rose around 26 percent in the April-June quarter compared to last year, driven up principally by a 94 percent increase in the oil bill. \par \par \uc2  While there are hopes the rate of increase in the oil bill will taper off in coming months due to lower global crude prices, analysts still expect the current account deficit to rise to around 2.0 percent of gross domestic product (GDP) in 2000/01 (April-March) from around 1.5 percent a year earlier. \par \par \uc2  And foreign funds have been heavy sellers recently, dumping equity worth nearly $550 million in June and July. \par \par \uc2  While funds have bought a net $51.6 million so far in August, analysts say it is too early to report whether the selling trend has been reversed. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark top-30 share index of the Bombay exchange is some 30 percent off its record high of 6,150.69 hit on February 14 although it is up some 13 percent from the year's low hit in May. ((Bombay newsroom, +91 22 265-9000 fax +91 22 264-1699, bombay.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dw8800bdv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
