\par\par \b \uc2  India Rupee Ends At A New Low Of INR45.74/Dlr (INR45.55) \b0\par\par\uc2 417 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 August 2000\par 12:32\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  (Corrected 1108GMT) \par \par \uc2  1032GMT \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee fell for the seventh consecutive session and ended at its lowest ever close against the dollar Tuesday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at INR45.74, up from INR45.55 Monday. \par \par \uc2  The rupee hit a new traded low of INR45.7450 to the dollar on sustained dollar-buying by companies in thin trade. \par \par \uc2  The Indian rupee has lost 5.2% of its value against the dollar in the past year. \par \par \uc2  Adding to the bearish sentiment was the absence of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Despite Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Bimal Jalan's reassurance Thursday, the rupee has continued to fall as demand for dollars continues to outstrip supply. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of India Thursday issued a statement urging the markets to view the rupee's fall in the perspective of the dollar's rise vis-a-vis other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Indian rupee opened on a bearish note Tuesday and weakened in early trade on sustained dollar-buying in an illiquid market. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been conducting repurchase auctions daily to mop up liquidity and deter banks from holding long-dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  Tuesday the Reserve Bank accepted INR36.45 billion of overnight funds at 14% and INR23.25 billion of three-day money at 15%. \par \par \uc2  However the tighter liquidity hasn't arrested the rupee's fall as most of the demand for the dollar is genuine demand from companies and not speculative. \par \par \uc2  Two years ago, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  imposed restrictions on corporate \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions making it difficult for them to take speculative positions. \par \par \uc2  However dealers said importer dollar demand is waning and exporters are beginning to show an interest in selling dollars. \par \par \uc2  In the forward market, rupee premiums on the forward dollar ended lower as most banks reversed their positions and few large banks sold forward dollars in the market. \par \par \uc2  January premiums ended at INR1.09, down from INR1.11 Monday, while July premiums ended at INR2.09, unchanged from Monday. \par \par \uc2  Looking ahead, dealers said demand for the dollar has begun to wane, adding that the rupee's current fall appears to be at an end. \par \par \uc2  "I think we are almost at the end of the fall," a dealer at a foreign bank said. \par \par \uc2  Rupee premiums are expected to move up if the Reserve Bank tightens liquidity further. \par \par \uc2  -By Ramya Venugopal, Dow Jones Newswires; 91-22-288-4211; ramya.venugopal@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1164 1847 62506 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw8803uzz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
