\par\par \b \uc2  WRAP:India RBI Comment Cheers Mkt,But Rupee To Fall More \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ramya Venugopal \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 383 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 2000\par 17:13\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY -(Dow Jones)- The market greeted with a mixture of relief and unease the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Thursday to boost the flagging rupee, bankers said. \par \par \uc2  While market participants expressed relief that the Reserve Bank didn't raise interest rates or impose restrictions on trading to stem the rupee's decline, it also means that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is left to its own devices and is therefore susceptible to further weakening, bankers said. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of India Thursday issued a statement urging the market to view the rupee's fall in the perspective of the dollar's rise against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , adding that the rupee strengthened against the euro and yen and was stable against sterling. \par \par \uc2  The Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has hit record lows for three consecutive sessions this week on sustained dollar-buying by state-owned companies, importers and foreign funds. The rupee fell to a new low of 45.44 Thursday, but recovered to end at 45.34, it lowest close. The Indian rupee has lost 1% of its value against the dollar this week. \par \par \uc2  In the past, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has raised short-term interest rates and imposed restrictions on bank trading to stem the rupee's fall. \par \par \uc2  "The statement (Thursday) reflects a paradigm shift in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's approach," said V. Ravi Kumar, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at ABN-Amro Bank in India. "It is an effort to calm the market and give a rationale to the developments." \par \par \uc2  Dealers believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is comfortable with the falling rupee and is only attempting to ease the panic-buying of dollars. \par \par \uc2  But the sustained dollar demand will continue to exert pressure on the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the rupee is expected to test INR45.50 against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "We expect the rupee to settle between INR45.30-INR45.50 for the time being," a dealer at a foreign bank said. \par \par \uc2  "But our recommendation to our clients will continue to be buy on dips and cover a part of your exposures," said a treasurer at a private sector bank. \par \par \uc2  -By Ramya Venugopal, Dow Jones Newswires; +91-22-288-4211; ramya.venugopal@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw8303t8g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
