\par\par \b \uc2  India Rupee Ends At Record Low INR45.03/Dlr (INR44.8750) \b0\par\par\uc2 437 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Juli 2000\par 12:32\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY -(Dow Jones)- The Indian rupee ended Monday at an all-time low against the dollar, falling to INR45.03 as dollar demand from state-owned banks triggered short covering by banks and buying by importers, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Indian unit had closed Friday at INR44.8750 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The rupee is approaching its all-time lowest traded level of INR45.08 to the dollar, which it touched on July 21. The Reserve Bank of India had then raised short term interest rates and arrested the rupee's fall. \par \par \uc2  The dollar opened higher, but was expected to cap at INR44.90, when State Bank of India bought dollars at INR44.9050, sending jitters through the market. \par \par \uc2  State Bank of India is the country's largest commercial bank and buys dollars on behalf of state-owned companies, including the oil majors. It is also seen as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's proxy in India's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said some banks had gone short on the dollar at INR44.90 Friday and buying by State Bank of India Monday led them to cover their positions. A few importers had been buying since early trade and this added to the pressure on the Indian unit. \par \par \uc2  The Indian unit breached INR45.00 and slipped to the day's low of INR45.03, before a state-owned bank sold dollars, leading other banks to book profits. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said these banks were selling at the behest of the Reserve Bank of India. \par \par \uc2  The Indian unit hovered between INR44.97 and INR45.03 for the rest of the session. \par \par \uc2  In the forward market, rupee premiums on the forward dollar moved up sharply in tandem with the spot dollar. \par \par \uc2  The premium on January dollars ended at INR0.94, up from INR0.90 Friday. July dollars ended at INR1.88, up from INR1.82 Friday. \par \par \uc2  Looking ahead, dealers said sentiment on the Indian rupee has turned weak and the dollar is seen moving up. \par \par \uc2  Tuesday, the dollar is expected to open higher, but the market will take its cue from the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "If the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  early Tuesday, through state-owned banks, we'll see range-bound trading, but the long-term outlook is a higher dollar," a senior dealer at a private bank said. \par \par \uc2  "We are looking at a new trading range of INR44.95 and INR45.10 for the next few weeks," the chief dealer at a foreign bank said. \par \par \uc2  -By Ramya Venugopal, Dow Jones Newswires; 91-22-288-4211; ramya.venugopal@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1164 1847 62506 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw7v03pjv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
