\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Indian  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  hikes rates to prop up rupee. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Vidya Ranganathan \par \uc2 666 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juli 2000\par 15:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, July 21 (Reuters) - The Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its key interest rate by one percentage point to eight percent and tightened liquidity on Friday to support the rupee after it hit record lows. \par \par \uc2  The rupee hit a new low on Friday, extending a two-week downtrend as foreign investors exiting the stock market converted their rupees into dollars. \par \par \uc2  It hit its weakest intra-day level of 45.075 per dollar before recovering on suspicion of covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) through state-run banks. \par \par \uc2  The rupee, convertible only on the current account, has slipped just under one percent in the past two weeks. \par \par \uc2  But it has depreciated by 3.5 percent since the start of the year against a U.S. dollar which has been flying high against other major international \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The RBI said it was hiking interest rates after reviewing recent developments in the international and domestic financial markets, including the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  It raised the key bank rate, at which it refinances banks, by one percentage point to eight percent, but simultaneously halved banks' refinance limits. \par \par \uc2  It also raised banks' cash reserve ratio (CRR) by 50 basis points to 8.5 percent - to be carried out in two stages, on July 29 and August 12. The measure will mop up 38 billion rupees from the banking system. \par \par \uc2  Bankers, industrialists and analysts were surprised by the sudden monetary tightening and hoped they were temporary. \par \par \uc2  "This is an unexpected move. It is a cause for concern since the RBI had reduced interest rates a few months ago. This does not spell good news for the stock market," said Sangeeta Purushottam, head of research at SG Asia Securities (India). \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said the rupee will recover sharply to 44.50 levels when the market opens on Monday. \par \par \uc2  R. Govindan, assistant general manager, treasury, Larsen & Toubro, said the rupee's weakness was more due to genuine dollar demand and the RBI could have responded by selling dollars. \par \par \uc2  "These money market measures may end up affecting the economy and industry. Of course, if they are short term it is okay," he said. \par \par \uc2  TAKES THE PRESSURE OFF \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\b0 \highlight\uc2  RESERVES \par \par \uc2  When the RBI lowered key interest rates at the start of the year, analysts said it would help push through an aggressive 1.17 trillion rupees government borrowing programme and encourage faster economic growth. \par \par \uc2  The first quarter saw a strong growth in bank credit, and a near 100 basis points rise in long-term government bond yields. \par \par \uc2  The RBI said it would not tighten liquidity to curb rising inflation and even during volatile swings in the rupee in May and June, it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  through warnings against speculation and by selling dollars. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are down $1.67 billion from all time highs of $38.34 in mid-April. \par \par \uc2  Analysts feel it is the concern over dwindling capital inflows and a likely drop in the balance of payments surplus that has prompted the RBI to tighten. \par \par \uc2  "The bank rate hike was expected. The economy is moving at a fast clip and the balance of payments pressure has resulted in a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ...after spending around $2 billion of reserve money this was the only resort left to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Aashish Pitale, head of research at J P Morgan. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors have so far sold $448 million worth of Indian stocks in June and July, and are believed to be the main drivers of the 10 percent fall in the benchmark Bombay stock index in the past two weeks. \par \par \uc2  The BSE index .BSESN) closed down 2.45 percent at 4,463.66 points on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Political concerns also weighed on the stock market. There were fears the possible arrest of Bal Thackeray, leader of the Hindu Shiv Sena party, would spark unrest in Bombay and unsettle the ruling coalition in which the party has three cabinet ministers. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw7l0a11z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
