\par\par \b \uc2  Indian Rupee Down Early; Bks Buy Dlrs Expecting Corp Demand \b0\par\par\uc2 221 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juli 2000\par 06:09\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY -(Dow Jones)- The Indian rupee is lower in early trade Tuesday as banks buy dollars in anticipation of demand from companies and foreign funds, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  At 0400 GMT (12:00 a.m. EDT), the dollar was at 44.73 rupees, up from late Monday's INR44.7175. \par \par \uc2  "Monday's dollar demand is expected to spill over today (Tuesday) and that is making the market slightly nervous," a dealer at a U.S. bank said. \par \par \uc2  The steady selling of Indian shares by foreign funds is expected to fuel dollar demand as the funds will likely convert some of their rupees to dollars and repatriate the proceeds, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Bankers said they expect the dollar to trade in the INR44.72 to INR44.75 range Tuesday. "But I think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will start supporting the rupee at INR44.74 itself," a dealer at a European bank said. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , usually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market through State Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the country's largest bank. \par \par \uc2  State Bank is quoting market rates for the dollar early Tuesday which indicates it isn't looking to protect the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  -By S. Anuradha, Dow Jones Newswires; 91-22-288-4211; su.anuradha@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1164 1847 62506 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw7i03h9c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
