\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee unlikely to weaken further - CRISIL. \b0\par\par\uc2 410 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juli 2000\par 11:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, July 5 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee which has lost over 2.5 percent against the dollar since the beginning of May is unlikely to weaken further, rating agency Credit Rating Information Services of India Ltd (CRISIL) said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "With the dollar's appreciating trend against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  showing signs of receding, and with domestic inflation stabilising, CRISIL believes that there do not appear to be any immediate reasons for the rupee to depreciate sharply from the current levels," the rating agency said. \par \par \uc2  It said the depreciation may only be gradual. \par \par \uc2  The Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  which is convertible on the current account touched an all time low of 44.95 per dollar on June 8 before verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) and persistent dollar sales by state-run banks helped the rupee to stabilise around the current levels of 44.63/69 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also restricted rupee supplies in the domestic money market to curb banks from going long on dollars. \par \par \uc2  The agency said the sharp depreciation in the rupee vis-a-vis the dollar was partly due to higher domestic inflation and the hardening of the U.S. dollar against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Inflation, based on the wholesale price index, in the current financial year (April-March) 2000/01 rose from 2.74 percent in the week ended March 4, 2000 to a high of 6.59 percent in the week ended May 27. \par \par \uc2  It has since stabilised around six percent and was at 6.28 percent for the week ended June 17, 2000. \par \par \uc2  Crisil said the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had to depreciate in a bid to maintain the country's export competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  "With rising domestic prices, measured by wholesale price index (WPI) inflation and foreign price levels remaining constant, this led to a relative price movement against India or a worsening trend in India's export competitiveness," CRISIL said. \par \par \uc2  The rating agency said while the rupee has been stable since October 1999, depreciating by only 0.5 percent between October 1999 to April 2000 it appreciated by 11.3 percent against the euro and the Deutche mark and by 4.1 percent against the pound during the same period. \par \par \uc2  India's major trading partners, apart from the U.S, are the European Union, United Kingdom and Japan. \par \par \uc2  ((Bombay newsroom, +91 22 265-9000, fax +91 22 264-1699, anirban.nag@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw7502fsy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
