\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee seen at $46.50-47.0 to dollar - study. \b0\par\par\uc2 292 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 2000\par 15:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW DELHI, June 10 (Reuters) - A leading Indian industry body on Saturday said that the rupee would fall further and stabilise between 46.50 rupees and 47 rupees to a U.S. dollar towards the end of 2000/2001 (April-March). \par \par \uc2  An Associated Chambers of Commerce and Industry of India (ASSOCHAM) study said the widening trade deficit and shrinking foreign direct investment inflows would put pressue on the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "....the sudden depreciation of the rupee in the recent weeks indicates that the bright spots built up in the external sector are suddenly losing lustre," the association said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the rupee closed at a record low of 44.76/78 to the dollar against 44.72/735 on Thursday, having lost 2.5 percent since early May. During May the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  with strong verbal warnings when the markets turned volatile. \par \par \uc2  Government data released earlier this month showed India's trade deficit leapt to $1.11 billion in April 2000, some 112 percent above the defifit in the same month a year ago. \par \par \uc2  The ASSOCHAM said that with enough \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves at the central banks disposal, the "RBI will certainly be able to reduce the volatility in the exchange rates and ward off speculators. But it is unlikely that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be able to stand on the way of the broad market trends." \par \par \uc2  India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves stood at $37.069 billion on June 2, according to latest RBI data. Reserves stood at $33.433 billion on June 4, 1999. \par \par \uc2  ((New Delhi newsroom, +91-11-301-2024 Fax +91-11-301-4043; delhi.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw6a03tuc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
