\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-Indian rupee rebounds, c.bank talks tough. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Vidya Ranganathan \par \uc2 514 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juni 2000\par 14:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, June 8 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee rebounded sharply from a record intra-day low on Thursday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  issued a strong warning to banks against speculation and asked them to unwind long dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  The market, which had been expecting some form of action by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , reacted by yanking the rupee from its record low of 44.95 to the dollar to 44.54/61. It closed at 44.72/735. \par \par \uc2  The volatility subsided, but the comments left markets wondering about the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's comfort with the rupee. \par \par \uc2  "If they (the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) indicate comfort levels, then that will bring in exporters and importers and automatically reinforce a range for the rupee. This way, one cannot even form a view," the chief dealer at a foreign bank said. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of India (RBI) has always maintained it is not against orderly depreciation but will curb volatility. It normally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  through select state-run banks. \par \par \uc2  "The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants a gradual slide in the rupee, so it is not surprising they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . I still expect the rupee will weaken to 45 over the next three days," a chief dealer at a European bank said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's warning was terse: \par \par \uc2  "The inter-bank activity this morning, despite RBI's repeated reminders, seem to be speculative in nature. RBI has instructed banks to maintain square position for the day," the statement read. \par \par \uc2  It is the second time in two weeks the RBI has moved to support the rupee. The bank last stepped in on May 25 when the rupee fell to a low of 44.75. Bbut it has maintained silence over the past two weeks as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gradually lost ground. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, Finance Minister Yashwant Sinha said he saw the pressure on the rupee as a temporary phenomenon. \par \par \uc2  India has enough \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to weather the pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said. \par \par \uc2  CORRECTION OVER, BUT SOME BEARS REMAIN \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the rupee's correction, engineered by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was over and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was no longer overvalued against its inflation-adjusted trade-weighted rate. \par \par \uc2  "What we are seeing right now is a healthy correction which will check out some of the short, volatile and speculative positions remaining in the dollar market," N. Balasubramanian, head of trading at ICICI Ltd told Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2  The rupee's sudden sharp fall on Thursday notwithstanding, the market predicted a gradual and marginal slide during the rest of the year. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters poll of 14 corporate treasurers and economists released on Thursday predicted the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would fall only 1.8 percent to 45.30 by December and to 45.57 by March 2001. \par \par \uc2  These levels are a big change from expectations at the start of the year when a Reuters poll had predicted the rupee at 44.02 by September 2000 and 44.54 by March 2001. \par \par \uc2  ((Bombay Treasury Desk; +91-22-265 9000; fax 91-22-264 1708; vidya.ranganathan@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw68040w5